                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 18-year-old female patient, admitted on 16/03/2025 with acute lower abdominal pain and a positive pregnancy test. Clinical examination and transvaginal ultrasound confirmed a right-sided tubal ectopic pregnancy without signs of rupture on initial presentation. Serial serum beta-hCG measurements supported the diagnosis. Given her hemodynamic stability, laparoscopic approach was planned; however, intraoperatively a ruptured tubal ectopic pregnancy was identified. She subsequently underwent laparoscopic salpingectomy with complete removal of the ectopic pregnancy. The postoperative course was uneventful. Wound care was provided and vital signs remained stable throughout. She was discharged home on 18/03/2025 in good condition. Counseling regarding future fertility and contraception was provided prior to discharge. Please ensure follow-up within two weeks.</div>"
            },
